sec.p:
Let the signal vector transmitted on k-th subcarrier and m-th OFDM symbol over Nt antennas be denoted by ${{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}} \in {{\mathbb{C}}^{{N_t} \times 1}}$. Applying Nt K-point inverse fast Fourier transform (IFFT) to the signal xk,m, and following an upconversion over the block of M symbols in t ∈ [0, MTsym], the transmit signal in time domain takes the form [23], [24]
\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{\tilde x}}(t) = \Re \left\{ {\sum\limits_{m = 1}^M {\sum\limits_{k = 1}^K {{{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}} } {e^{j2\pi {f_k}t}}\operatorname{rect} \left({\frac{{t - m{T_{{\text{sym}}}}}}{{{T_{{\text{sym}}}}}}}\right)} \right\}\tag{1}\end{equation*}
where the rectangular pulse function rect(t) takes the value 1 for t ∈ [0, 1] and 0 otherwise, fc is the carrier frequency and fk = fc+k△f is the k-th subcarrier frequency.

With ${\tilde x_{{n_t}}}(t)$ denotng the nt-th entry of ${\mathbf{\tilde x}}(t)$ in (1), the received signal at nr-th receive antenna after propagation delay is
\begin{equation*}{y_{{n_r}}}(t) = \sum\limits_{q = 1}^Q {{\alpha _q}} g_{{n_r}}^q(t)\sum\limits_{{n_t} = 1}^{{N_t}} {g_{{n_t}}^q} (t){\tilde x_{{n_t}}}\left({t - \tau _{{n_t}{n_r}}^q}\right)\cdot{e^{j2\pi f_{{n_t}{n_r}}^{q,k}t}} + {n_{{n_r}}}(t)\tag{2}\end{equation*}
where αq denotes the RCS of q-th target and ${n_{{n_r}}}(t)$ is the front end additive Gaussian white noise (AWGN) of the nr-th Rx element. The Doppler shift, $f_{{n_t}{n_r}}^{q,k}$, for the q-th target on the k-th subcarrier with the nt-th Tx and nr-th Rx antenna pair is given [25]
\begin{equation*}f_{{n_t}{n_r}}^{q,k} = \frac{{{f_k}}}{c}\left({\frac{{\left\langle {{{\mathbf{v}}^{(q)}},{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_{{n_t}}}} \right\rangle }}{{\left\| {{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_{{n_t}}}} \right\|}} + \frac{{\left\langle {{{\mathbf{v}}^{(q)}},{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_{{n_r}}}} \right\rangle }}{{\left\| {{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_{{n_r}}}} \right\|}}}\right),\tag{3}\end{equation*}
where ${{\mathbf{v}}^{(q)}} = \left[ {v_x^{(q)},v_y^{(q)}} \right]$ denotes the directional velocity of q-th target in which $v_x^{(q)}\;{\text{and}}\;v_y^{(q)}$ are the velocity along with x and y-axis, respectively [26]. Meanwhile, the time delay in (2) takes the form
\begin{equation*}\tau _{{n_t}{n_r}}^q = \frac{{\left\| {{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_{{n_t}}}} \right\| + \left\| {{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_{{n_r}}}} \right\|}}{c}.\tag{4}\end{equation*}

Here, aT is the wideband near-field Tx steering vector of Tx; the Rx steering vector is similarly defined. Further, φl(k, m, q) denotes the total phase shift caused by the location and Doppler of q-th target with respect to l-th element of Tx/ Rx at the k-th subcarrier of m-th OFDM symbol. For notation simplification, the receive signal in (6) is rewritten as
\begin{equation*}{{\mathbf{y}}_{k,m}} = {{\mathbf{A}}_{k,m}}{{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}} + {{\mathbf{n}}_{k,m}},m \in [1,M],k \in [1,K],\tag{7}\end{equation*}
where ${{\mathbf{A}}_{k,m}} = {{\mathbf{A}}_{{\text{R}},k,m}}{\mathbf{BA}}_{{\text{T}},k,m}^T$ denotes the channel response matrix, AT,k,m = [aT(k, m, 1), ⋯ , aT(k, m,Q)] and AR,k,m is similarly defined. Further, the diagonal matrix B = diag[α1, ⋯, αQ]. We then stack the received signal (7) from all M OFDM symbols into a single vector as
\begin{equation*}{{\mathbf{y}}_k} = {{\mathbf{A}}_k}{{\mathbf{x}}_k} + {{\mathbf{n}}_k} \in {{\mathbb{C}}^{{N_r}M}}\tag{8}\end{equation*}
where ${{\mathbf{y}}_k} = {\left[ {{\mathbf{y}}_{k,1}^T, \cdots ,{\mathbf{y}}_{k,M}^T} \right]^T}$ denotes the receive signal of k-th subcarrier with the mean μk = Akxk and the covariance ${{\mathbf{C}}_k} = \sigma _r^2{{\mathbf{I}}_{{N_r}M}}$ respectively, Ak = bdiag[Ak,1, ⋯,Ak,M], ${{\mathbf{x}}_k} = {\left[ {{\mathbf{x}}_{k,1}^T, \cdots ,{\mathbf{x}}_{k,M}^T} \right]^T}{\text{and}}\,{{\mathbf{n}}_k} = {\left[ {{\mathbf{n}}_{k,1}^T, \cdots ,{\mathbf{n}}_{k,M}^T} \right]^T}$. According to (8), the target-related real-valued unknown parameter is
\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{\theta }} = {\left[ {{\mathbf{x}},{\mathbf{y}},{{\mathbf{v}}_x},{{\mathbf{v}}_y},{{\mathbf{\alpha }}_R},{{\mathbf{\alpha }}_I}} \right]^T} \in {{\mathbb{R}}^{6Q \times 1}},\tag{9}\end{equation*}
where ${\mathbf{x}} = \left[ {{x_1}, \cdots ,{x_Q}} \right],{\mathbf{y}} = \left[ {{y_1}, \cdots ,{y_Q}} \right],{{\mathbf{v}}_x} = \left[ {v_x^{(1)}, \cdots ,v_x^{(Q)}} \right],{{\mathbf{v}}_y} = \left[ {v_y^{(1)}, \cdots ,v_y^{(Q)}} \right],{{\mathbf{\alpha }}_R} = \left[ {{\alpha _{{R_1}}}, \cdots ,{\alpha _{{R_Q}}}} \right]$, and ${\alpha _I} = \left[ {{\alpha _{{I_1}}}, \cdots ,{\alpha _{{I_Q}}}} \right]$. We will derive and analyze CRB for retrieving the parameter vector (9) from the received signal (8) in the sequel.

Then, FIM at k-th subcarrier is given by [29]
\begin{equation*}{{\mathbf{F}}_k} = - {\mathbb{E}}\left\{ {\frac{{{\partial ^2}\ln {p_k}\left({{{\overline {\mathbf{y}} }_k},{\mathbf{\theta }}}\right)}}{{\partial {\mathbf{\theta }}\partial {{\mathbf{\theta }}^T}}}} \right\}\tag{12}\end{equation*}
where ${\mathbb{E}}\{ \cdot \} $ denotes the expectation operator. Due to use of OFDM signaling and the assumption that Doppler does not create inter-carrier interference, the channel response and transmitted signals in different subcarriers can be treated as orthogonal. Thus, we can define the wideband FIM as [17]
\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{\tilde F}} = \sum\limits_{k = 1}^K {{{\mathbf{F}}_k}} .\tag{13}\end{equation*}

Based on Slepian-Bangs formula, we can calculate the inverse of generalized CRB on k-th subcarrier, see equation (B.3.25) in [29], as
\begin{equation*}{{\mathbf{F}}_k}(i,j) = \operatorname{Tr} \left({{\mathbf{C}}_k^{ - 1}\frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{C}}_k}}}{{\partial {\theta _i}}}{\mathbf{C}}_k^{ - 1}\frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{C}}_k}}}{{\partial {\theta _j}}}}\right) + 2\Re \left[ {{{\left({\frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{\mu }}_k}}}{{\partial {\theta _i}}}}\right)}^H}{\mathbf{C}}_k^{ - 1}\frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{\mu }}_k}}}{{\partial {\theta _j}}}} \right],\tag{14}\end{equation*}
in which the first term is equal to zero due to $\frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{C}}_k}}}{{\partial {\theta _k}}} = {\mathbf{0}},\forall k.$ Now, in wideband near-field case, the difficulty of FIM calculation lies in the derivation of (14). Towards this, the FIM for the k-th subcarrier is
\begin{equation*}{{\mathbf{F}}_k} = \left[ {\begin{array}{llllll} {{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{xx}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{xy}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{x}}{{\mathbf{v}}_x}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{x}}{{\mathbf{v}}_y}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{x}}{\alpha _R}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{x}}{\alpha _I}}^k} \\ {{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{yx}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{yy}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{y}}{{\mathbf{v}}_x}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{y}}{{\mathbf{v}}_y}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{y}}{\alpha _R}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\mathbf{y}}{\alpha _I}}^k} \\ {{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_x}{\mathbf{x}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_x}{\mathbf{y}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_x}{{\mathbf{v}}_x}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_x}{\mathbf{v}}y}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_x}{\alpha _R}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_x}{\alpha _I}}^k} \\ {{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_y}{\text{x}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_y}{\mathbf{y}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_y}{{\mathbf{v}}_x}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_y}{{\mathbf{v}}_y}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_y}{\alpha _R}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{{\mathbf{v}}_y}{\alpha _I}}^k} \\ {{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _R}{\text{x}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _R}{\mathbf{y}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _R}{{\mathbf{v}}_x}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _R}{{\mathbf{v}}_y}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _R}{\alpha _R}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _R}{\alpha _I}}^k} \\ {{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _{{I^{\text{x}}}}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _I}{\mathbf{y}}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _I}{{\mathbf{v}}_x}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _I}{{\mathbf{v}}_y}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _I}{\alpha _R}}^k}&{{\mathbf{F}}_{{\alpha _I}{\alpha _I}}^k} \end{array}} \right]\tag{15}\end{equation*}
in which the diagonal blocks are related to the target position, velocity, and RCS estimation, respectively. The matrix of CRB is given by ${{\mathbf{B}}_k} = {\mathbf{F}}_k^{ - 1}$. Due to the nature of the CRB, an exact analysis is not possible and one needs numerical evaluation. In this work, to gain further insights, we use the Schur’s complement method to calculate the inverse of Fk and then derive the following lower bound ${{\mathbf{B}}_k}(i,i) \geq {\mathbf{F}}_k^{ - 1}(i,i);$ See [30].

According to Cauchy-Riemann equations [31], we have
\begin{align*}&\frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{A}}_{k,m}}{{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}}}{{\partial {{\mathbf{\alpha }}_R}}} = \left[ {{{{\mathbf{\mathop{A}\limits^{\smile} }}}_{k,m}}(1){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}, \cdots ,{{{\mathbf{\mathop{A}\limits^{\smile} }}}_{k,m}}(Q){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}} \right],\tag{17a} \\ & \frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{A}}_{k,m}}{{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}}}{{\partial {{\mathbf{\alpha }}_I}}} = \left[ {j{{{\mathbf{\mathop{A}\limits^{\smile} }}}_{k,m}}(1){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}, \cdots ,j{{{\mathbf{\mathop{A}\limits^{\smile} }}}_{k,m}}(Q){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}} \right],\tag{17b}\end{align*}
where ${{\mathbf{\mathop{A}\limits^{\smile} }}_{k,m}}(q) = {{\mathbf{A}}_{{\text{R}},k,m}}{{\mathbf{\Lambda }}_q}{\mathbf{A}}_{{\text{T}},k,m}^T$ denotes the partial derivative matrix and Λq denotes the selection matrix with q-th diagonal element is one and the others are zero. Substituting (17) into (13) and (14), we can get the wideband CRB for RCS estimation as [17]
\begin{equation*}{\text{CR}}{{\text{B}}_{{\alpha _{{R_q}}},L}} = {\operatorname{CRB} _{{\alpha _{{I_q}}},L}} = \frac{{\sigma _r^2}}{{2\sum _{k = 1}^K\sum _{m = 1}^MG_{{\text{R}},k}^q\left\| {{\mathbf{a}}_{\text{T}}^T(k,m,q){{\text{x}}_{k,m}}} \right\|_2^2}},\tag{18}\end{equation*}
where $G_{{\text{R}},k}^q = \left\| {{{\mathbf{a}}_{\text{R}}}(k,m,q)} \right\|_2^2$ denotes the Rx antenna gain on k-th subcarrier.

CRB for Velocity Estimation: Similarly, the velocity part is
\begin{align*}&\frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{A}}_{k,m}}{{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}}}{{\partial {{\mathbf{v}}_x}}} = \left[ {{{\mathbf{\acute{A}}}_{k,m}}(1){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}, \cdots ,{{\mathbf{\acute{A}}}_{k,m}}(Q){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}} \right],\tag{19a} \\ & \frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{A}}_{k,m}}{{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}}}{{\partial {{\mathbf{v}}_y}}} = \left[ {{{\mathop {\mathbf{A}}\limits^` }_{k,m}}(1){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}, \cdots ,{{\mathop {\mathbf{A}}\limits^` }_{k,m}}(Q){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}} \right],\tag{19b}\end{align*}
where ${{\mathbf{\acute{A}}}_{k,m}}(q) = \left[ {{{\mathbf{\acute{A}}}_{{\text{R}},k,m}}{{\mathbf{\Lambda }}_q}{\mathbf{BA}}_{{\text{T}},k,m}^T + {{\mathbf{A}}_{{\text{R}},k,m}}{\mathbf{B}}{{\mathbf{\Lambda }}_q}{\mathbf{\acute{A}}}_{{\text{T}},k,m}^T} \right]$ and Àk,m(q) denotes the partial derivative of Ak,m w.r.t. ${v_{{x_q}}}$ and ${v_{{y_q}}}$, respectively, in which any of these is given
\begin{align*}&\frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{a}}_{{\text{R}},{\text{T}},*}}(k,m,q)}}{{\partial {v_{{x_q}}}}} = \frac{{j2\pi \frac{{{f_k}}}{c}m{T_{{\text{sym}}}}\left({{x_q} - {x_*}}\right)}}{{\left\| {{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_*}} \right\|}}{{\mathbf{a}}_{{\text{R}},{\text{T}},*}}(k,m,q)\tag{20a} \\ & \frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{a}}_{{\text{R}},{\mathbf{T}},*}}(k,m,q)}}{{\partial {v_{{y_q}}}}} = \frac{{j2\pi \frac{{{f_k}}}{c}m{T_{{\text{sym}}}}\left({{y_q} - {y_*}}\right)}}{{\left\| {{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_*}} \right\|}}{{\mathbf{a}}_{{\text{R}},{\text{T}},*}}(k,m,q).\tag{20b}\end{align*}

CRB for Position Estimation: Similarly, we have
\begin{align*}&\frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{A}}_{k,m}}{{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}}}{{\partial {\mathbf{x}}}} = \left[ {{{\ddot{\mathbf{A}} }_{k,m}}(1){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}, \cdots ,{{\ddot{\mathbf{A}} }_{k,m}}(Q){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}} \right],\tag{22a} \\ & \frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{A}}_{k,m}}{{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}}}{{\partial {\mathbf{y}}}} = \left[ {{{\mathop {\mathbf{A}}\limits^. }_{k,m}}(1){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}, \cdots ,{{\mathop {\mathbf{A}}\limits^. }_{k,m}}(Q){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}} \right],\tag{22b}\end{align*}
where ${\ddot{\mathbf{A}}_{k,m}}(q) = \left[ {{{\ddot{\mathbf{A}} }_{{\text{R}},k,m}}{{\mathbf{\Lambda }}_q}{\mathbf{BA}}_{{\text{T}},k,m}^T + {{\mathbf{A}}_{{\text{R}},k,m}}{\mathbf{B}}{{\mathbf{\Lambda }}_q}\ddot{\mathbf{A}} _{{\text{T}},k,m}^T} \right]$ and ${\dot{\mathbf{A}}_{k,m}}(q) = \left[ {{{\mathop {\mathbf{A}}\limits^. }_{{\text{R}},k,m}}{{\mathbf{\Lambda }}_q}{\mathbf{BA}}_{{\text{T}},k,m}^T + {{\mathbf{A}}_{{\text{R}},k,m}}{\mathbf{B}}{{\mathbf{\Lambda }}_q}\dot{\mathbf{A}}_{{\text{T}},k,m}^T} \right]$ denotes the partial derivative of Ak,m w.r.t. xq and yq, respectively, with each element of the derivative w.r.t xq being $\frac{{\partial {{\mathbf{a}}_{{\text{R}},{\text{T}}, * }}(k,m,q)}}{{\partial {x_q}}} = {\ddot g_{{\text{R}},{\text{T}}, * }}(k,m,q){{\mathbf{a}}_{{\text{R}},{\text{T}}, * }}(k,m,q){\text{,}}$ where the gain ${\ddot g_{{\text{R}},{\text{T}},*}}(k,m,q)$ is given by (23) arranged at the top of next page and each element of the derivation w.r.t yq can be similarly obtained. Substituting (22) into (14) and (13), lower bound on CRB of the location estimation for q-th target is
\begin{align*}&{\ddot g_{{\text{R}},{\text{T}},*}}(k,m,q) = \left({\frac{{{\lambda _k}\left({{x_*} - {x_q}}\right)}}{{4\pi {{\left\| {{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_*}} \right\|}^2}}} + \frac{{j2\pi \frac{{{f_k}}}{c}\left({v_x^{(q)}m{T_{{\text{sym }}}} - \left({{x_q} - {x_*}}\right)}\right)}}{{\left\| {{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_*}} \right\|}} - } \right.\left. {\frac{{j2\pi \frac{{{f_k}}}{c}m{T_{{\text{sym}}}}\left({v_x^{(q)}{{\left({{x_q} - {x_*}}\right)}^2} + v_y^{(q)}\left({{x_q} - {x_*}}\right)\left({{y_q} - {y_*}}\right)}\right)}}{{{{\left\| {{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_*}} \right\|}^3}}}}\right).\tag{23} \\ {\operatorname{CRB} _{{x_q},L}} = \frac{{\sigma _r^2}}{{2\sum _{k = 1}^K\sum _{m = 1}^M\left\| {{{\ddot{\mathbf{A}} }_{k,m}}(q){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}} \right\|_2^2}},\tag{24a} \\ & {\text{CR}}{{\text{B}}_{{y_q},L}} = \frac{{\sigma _r^2}}{{2\sum _{k = 1}^K\sum _{m = 1}^M\left\| {{{\mathop {\mathbf{A}}\limits^. }_{k,m}}(q){{\mathbf{x}}_{k,m}}} \right\|_2^2}}.\tag{24b}\end{align*}

First, for the far-field case, we can approximate the distance between target and each elements as equal, i.e., ${r_q} = \left\| {{{\mathbb{I}}_q} - {{\mathbb{I}}_o}} \right\|$, where ${{\mathbb{I}}_o}$ denotes the location of first reference element. Thus, for RCS estimation, the CRB under far-field case is given
\begin{align*} & {\text{CRB}}_{\alpha ,L}^{{\text{NFF}}} \approx \frac{{256\sigma _r^2{\pi ^4}r_{\text{T}}^2r_{\text{R}}^2}}{{{\mathcal{P}}M{N_t}{N_r}\lambda _c^4}},\tag{25} \\ & {\text{CRB}}_{\alpha ,L}^{{\text{WFF}}} \approx \frac{{256\sigma _r^2{\pi ^4}r_{\text{T}}^2r_{\text{R}}^2}}{{{\mathcal{P}}M{N_t}{N_r}\sum _{k = 1}^K\lambda _k^4}},\tag{26}\end{align*}
where rT and rR denote the distance from target to first element of Tx and Rx, respectively. Then, we consider the near-field and ELAA case, if the Tx and Rx deployed alone to x-axis with the first element at the origin, and the target located at y-axis, then we can approximate the near-field CRB for RCS as
\begin{align*}&{\text{CRB}}_{\alpha ,L}^{{\text{NNF}}} \approx \frac{{256\sigma _r^2{\pi ^4}\hat r_{\text{T}}^2\hat r_{\text{R}}^2}}{{{\mathcal{P}}M{N_t}{N_r}\lambda _c^4}},\tag{27a} \\ & {\text{CRB}}_{\alpha ,L}^{{\text{WNF}}} \approx \frac{{256\sigma _r^2{\pi ^4}\hat r_{\text{T}}^2\hat r_{\text{R}}^2}}{{{\mathcal{P}}M{N_t}{N_r}\sum _{k = 1}^K\lambda _k^4}},\tag{27b}\end{align*}
where ${\hat r_{\text{T}}}\;{\text{and}}\;{\hat r_{\text{R}}}$ denote the distance between target to the centroid of Tx and Rx, respectively. Based on (27b), we have the following asymptotic behavior for wideband near-field CRB
\begin{align*}&\mathop {\lim }\limits_{{\mathcal{P}} \to \infty } {\text{CRB}}_{\alpha ,L}^{{\text{WNF}}} = 0;\mathop {\lim }\limits_{K \to \infty } {\text{CRB}}_{\alpha ,L}^{{\text{WNF}}} = 0;\tag{28a} \\ & \mathop {\lim }\limits_{{N_r} \to \infty } {\text{CRB}}_{\alpha ,L}^{{\text{WNF}}} = 0;\mathop {\lim }\limits_{{N_t} \to \infty } {\text{CRB}}_{\alpha ,L}^{{\text{WNF}}} = 0,\tag{28b} \\ & \mathop {\lim }\limits_{M \to \infty } {\text{CRB}}_{\alpha ,L}^{{\text{WNF}}} = 0;\mathop {\lim }\limits_{{\lambda _k} \to \infty } {\text{CRB}}_{\alpha ,L}^{{\text{WNF}}} = 0.\tag{28c}\end{align*}
